  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "In this scene focused on the lost key, the kitchen staff feigns ignorance about the lost key until Chef produces a spare. Let's listen closely to Chef Viktor and the staff's exchange in Russian first, and then walk through each line together.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Никто не видел запасного ключа?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "А мы не знаем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "И не видели.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Но вообще...",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Ничем не смогу вам помочь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Мои сотрудники потеряли ключ.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Экая досада.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ой, ой, ой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "А как же нам быть?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У меня есть запасной ключ.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я всегда дубликаты храню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А, вот он, ключ.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Нашелся ключ?",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Никто не видел запасного ключа?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Has anyone seen the spare key?' Chef Viktor puts 'nobody' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'saw'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "А мы не знаем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We do not know.' Notice the word order: 'we' comes first, and 'know' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "И не видели.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And we have not seen it.' Here 'saw' uses the plural past-tense ending, which also serves for the polite 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Но вообще...",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But, generally speaking...' You'll hear 'at all' all the time in the show, here connected with 'at all'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Ничем не смогу вам помочь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am afraid I cannot help you.' The first content word means 'by nothing', and 'to help' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Мои сотрудники потеряли ключ.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'My staff lost the key.' the speaker starts with 'employees' and pairs it with 'lost' to make the point clear. Chef Viktor answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Экая досада.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What a shame.' Chef Viktor puts 'what a' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'annoyance / shame'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ой, ой, ой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Oh, oh, oh.' In Russian, 'oh' leads into 'oh' in a very natural everyday phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "А как же нам быть?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'So what are we to do?' The first content word means 'how', and 'be' rounds out the thought. Then Chef Viktor replies.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У меня есть запасной ключ.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I have a spare key.' A compact expression built around 'me' and 'key'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я всегда дубликаты храню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I always keep a duplicate.' In Russian, 'I' leads into 'to keep' in a very natural everyday phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А, вот он, ключ.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Ah, there is the key.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'he' straight to 'key'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Нашелся ключ?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Found the key?' the speaker starts with 'to turn up / be found' and pairs it with 'key' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Никто не видел запасного ключа?",
